Scans for Wi-Fi networks hourly, not every five minutes, for a city.
At city accuracy GeoClue asks wpa_supplicant for a scan five minutes
after each one ends, for as long as a client wants a position. A scan
takes the card off its channel for five to six seconds, over every
channel it allows, and wpa_supplicant weighs roaming after any scan:
on a laptop that sat still at a desk, each of its four roams in an
evening came within 20 ms of one of these scans, flipping between two
radios of the same access point, and each cost the link 110 to 180 ms.
A city doesn't move
in five minutes, and GeoClue already refreshes its position on the
ScanDone of a scan anyone asked for, as wpa_supplicant makes one on
joining a network or roaming, so a new place is found as the card
joins a network there. Nothing in it is particular to this desktop.
--- a/src/gclue-wifi.c
+++ b/src/gclue-wifi.c
@@ -29,10 +29,15 @@
 #include "gclue-mozilla.h"
 
 #define WIFI_SCAN_TIMEOUT_HIGH_ACCURACY 10
-/* Since this is only used for city-level accuracy, 5 minutes between each
- * scan is more than enough.
+/* Since this is only used for city-level accuracy, an hour between each
+ * scan is enough. A scan takes the card off its channel for seconds, and
+ * wpa_supplicant may roam after any scan, so one every 5 minutes cost a
+ * connected laptop that sat still more than its position was worth. The
+ * scans wpa_supplicant makes itself, as it joins or roams, reach us as
+ * ScanDone in between, so a new place is still found as soon as the card
+ * joins a network there.
  */
-#define WIFI_SCAN_TIMEOUT_LOW_ACCURACY  300
+#define WIFI_SCAN_TIMEOUT_LOW_ACCURACY  3600
 
 /* WiFi APs at and below this signal level in scan results are ignored.
  * In dBm units.
